# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Docker build context
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The Dockerfile runs `COPY . .`, so without this file the entire working
# directory — including `.env` — is sent to the daemon and copied into the build
# stage.
#
# That matters for two reasons, and the first one is not about speed:
#
#   1. `.env` holds REVALIDATE_SECRET and the WordPress application password.
#      Baking it into a build layer is exactly the class of leak that
#      src/lib/env.ts exists to prevent on the application side; the build
#      context is the other half of the same problem.
#
#   2. `node_modules` is hundreds of megabytes. Sending it as context makes
#      every build slow, and `COPY . .` would then shadow the `npm ci` layer
#      the Dockerfile deliberately installs first — so a dependency change
#      would stop invalidating the cache correctly.
#
# Keep this list in sync with what the build actually needs: package.json,
# package-lock.json, src/, public/, scripts/, astro.config.mjs, tsconfig.json.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- secrets ---------------------------------------------------------------
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# --- installed / generated -------------------------------------------------
node_modules
node_modules.*
dist
dist.*
.astro
.astro.*
.lighthouse

# --- not needed to build the frontend --------------------------------------
.git
.github
.gitignore
screenshots
wordpress
.workbuddy-ai
.vscode
.idea

# NOTE: `deploy/` is deliberately NOT excluded. Docker reads the Dockerfile from
# the context (here, `deploy/Dockerfile`), and excluding its directory makes the
# build fail with a confusing "cannot locate Dockerfile" error. It is a few KB.

# --- noise -----------------------------------------------------------------
*.log
*.tmp
out.txt
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
